 "asked_at": "2026-10-17T08:45+08:00",
 "answer": "**Yes, the haze in Kuala Lumpur is quite bad right now.** The air quality is currently sitting in the **Unhealthy to Very Unhealthy** range.\n\n**Current Readings:**\n* **Official Malaysian API (APIMS):** Morning readings from the Department of Environment show **Unhealthy** levels across the Klang Valley, with **Cheras at 160**, **Batu Muda at 158**, and nearby **Putrajaya at 161**. \n* **International Trackers (US AQI):** Real-time PM2.5 trackers (like IQAir and AQICN) are reporting even higher localized spikes in parts of the city, ranging between **170 (Unhealthy)** and **202–212 (Very Unhealthy)**.\n\n**What's happening:**\nThe Klang Valley—along with much of Peninsular Malaysia, especially Johor—is currently experiencing a severe regional haze crisis driven by forest and peatland fires in Indonesia, carried across the strait by monsoon winds. Visibility across the city is significantly reduced.\n\n**Precautions to take:**\n* **Limit outdoor activities:** Avoid prolonged or strenuous exercise outside, especially if you belong to a sensitive group (children, the elderly, or those with asthma/respiratory issues).\n* **Wear a mask:** If you need to be outdoors for an extended period, wear a well-fitted **N95 or KN95 mask**, as standard surgical or cloth masks do not effectively filter out fine PM2.5 particles.\n* **Keep indoor air clean:** Keep windows and doors closed, and run an air purifier if you have one. \n\nFor real-time hourly updates by neighborhood, you can check the official [Malaysian DOE APIMS website](https://eqms.doe.gov.my/).",
